Z||000000||Formula 18||01-08-2001||11:15 PM||||I asked this once before but never got a response..... <P>What's the compresion ratio when you use iron heads on a block that came with AL heads?? <P>------------------<BR>Brian<BR> -88 Formula||||reg||1
Z||000001||jerpro||01-08-2001||11:41 PM||||it is somewhere between 0 and 400  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||2birds||01-09-2001||12:19 AM||||A lot higher, since the original aluminum heads are open chamber, with canted valves, and the pistons were domed to compensate.  That's according to my GM 60 degree V6 book. <p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 01-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||01-09-2001||03:52 AM||||You have it backwards, 2birds.  It's a bit lower.  The combustion chamber on the AL heads is smaller than the iron heads, so they had to go to dished pistons to keep the c/r down.  that's according to my GM 60* book   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I've heard it's around 8:1.  You can bump it up by using thinner head gaskets and having the block milled a little.  Can't give you an accurate number, that's just what I've heard.<P>Now, if you put AL heads on a block that came with iron heads, the c/r would be a lot higher. <p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 01-09-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000004||2birds||01-09-2001||11:08 AM||||I guess that's what I get for relying on memory.  Thanks, my frozen friend.||||reg||1
